<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
  <title>Family and Community as Context in Adolescence</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00061</md:content-id>
    <md:uuid>9a204e8f-e3ec-4410-85e3-06dd6882bd4c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="learning-objectives" id="sect-00001">
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>
    <para id="para-00001">By the end of this section, you will be able to:</para>
    <list id="list-00001">
    <item>Describe the features of various parenting styles</item>
    <item>Identify the sources of parent-adolescent conflict</item>
    <item>Discuss the features of the positive youth development perspective</item>
    </list>
    </section>
    <para id="para-00002">Fifteen-year-old Michael wakes up 20 minutes early each day so he can help his younger brother get dressed for school. While his mother is downstairs hurriedly stirring eggs on the stove while getting dressed for work and feeding the dog, Michael reminds his brother to make his bed before he heads downstairs. After breakfast Michael’s mother looks for the car keys. But Michael is already outside, having taken the keys to warm up the car on this wintry morning. Later that morning, while in school, Michael receives a text from his mom: “Thank you for helping us this morning. Pizza for dinner? I love you!” After school, the brothers go to the afterschool program offered by their church. Michael does homework while his brother plays with his friends in the gym. Both are excited to see their mother’s car pull up shortly after 5 pm. Time for pizza!</para>
    <para id="para-00003">Family life is a central feature in the lives of adolescents, serving as the anchor for their experiences. In this section, we’ll explore the important role that parents and family structure play in teenager’s lives, and then look at positive influences available in the broader community “family.”</para>
    <section id="sect-00002">
    <title>The Influence of Parenting Styles</title>
    <para id="para-00004">Although adolescence is a time of gradual turning toward the broader society, parents <link target-id="fig-00001"/> are still obviously an important source of support and guidance in adolescents’ lives. Recall the four main clusters of parenting styles – authoritative (high responsiveness, high expectations/guidance), indulgent or permissive (high responsiveness, low guidance/expectations), authoritarian (low responsiveness, high guidance/expectations) and neglectful or uninvolved (low responsiveness, low guidance/expectations).</para>
    <para id="para-00005">The authoritative parenting style is associated with many positive outcomes for adolescents, as it is for children. These outcomes include a more positive self-concept, more positive orientation toward school and academic competence, and fewer problem behaviors (Dagnew, 2015; Febiyanti &amp; Rachmawati, 2021; Steinberg, et al., 1994). In a one-year follow-up, researchers found these outcomes held steady, explaining that parenting style at that point helped maintain adolescents’ positive adjustment (Steinberg et al., 1994). Another study examined the relationships between parenting styles, academic self-concept, academic motivation, and academic achievement among Ethiopian secondary school students. The findings showed that the authoritative parenting style had a positive correlation with academic achievement, while authoritarian and permissive styles had negative correlations; there was no significant relationship with the neglectful style. Academic self-concept, intrinsic motivation, and extrinsic motivation were all positively correlated with academic achievement (Dagnew, 2015). As you might expect, the opposite pattern was found for the neglectful parenting style, and the outcomes for adolescents of authoritarian and permissive styles fell somewhere between.</para>
    <figure id="fig-00001">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!--<image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_10_05_Family.jpg"/>-->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>[Caption placeholder]</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00006">Maccoby and Martin (1983) A wealth of studies sought to replicate and extend these findings, identifying two underlying dimensions that help explain how parenting style is related to adolescent outcomes: warmth/responsiveness (level of responsiveness to the child’s needs) and demandingness (level of demands for age-appropriate behavior). First, given that a central developmental task for adolescents is to assume control and responsibility for their own behavior, called developing behavioral autonomy, it makes sense that the responsiveness and clearly communicated expectations in authoritative parenting create an ideal environment for this task. Parental authority is respected, even as the teen increases their own sense of behavioral autonomy. Second, the expectations of authoritarian parenting can come across as unwelcome and interfere with growing behavioral autonomy (Bi et al., 2018).</para>
    <note class="it-depends" id="note-00001">
    <title>Can Your Parent Be Your Friend?</title>
    <para id="para-00007">An intriguing question is whether a parent can or even should be considered a “friend” of their child. On one hand, we might say yes. Parents and children are in a very close, emotionally significant relationship and spend a lot of time together. From another perspective, we could say no because parents and children play very different roles, with different responsibilities, concerns, and amount of power.</para>
    <para id="para-00008">Baumrind’s research on parenting styles can help us make sense of this question by focusing on what is in the best interest of the child or adolescent. As we’ve discussed in this chapter, the authoritative parenting style is considered the most conducive to supporting appropriate development of children and adolescents. The winning combination of clear expectations and guidelines for behavior, communicated within a warm and loving relationship, helps developing adolescents understand and internalize the underlying reasons for the expectations. Researchers have found that teens actually prefer to have guidance, because it gives them a sense of security and direction in a rather complex world (Collins &amp; Steinberg, 2006; Elkind, 1998).</para>
    <para id="para-00009">The permissive style of parenting includes the warmth and daily involvement of the authoritative style but lacks the necessary guidance. Permissive parents often see themselves as friends of their children and are uncomfortable about or unwilling to set limits, perhaps to avoid disappointing their child or upsetting them. Although this avoids conflict in the moment, the developing teenager is left with little guidance and faces a bewildering number of decisions and scenarios across adolescence without parental guardrails (Elkind, 1998).</para>
    <para id="para-00010">Thus, developmental psychology is clear. Adolescents and younger children do well when parents give them both warmth and structure. The way parents achieve this balance can differ based on their cultural background and various factors in the child’s environment.</para>
    </note>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00003">
    <title>Parent-Teen Conflict</title>
    <para id="para-00011">A common notion about adolescence is that a high level of stress or conflict always arises between teens and parents. While hormonal fluctuations can play a small role in the day-to-day moods of developing teens, such temporary irritability can’t account for the full nature of the conflict developmental psychologists have studied. Strife between teens and parents [link]Figure 10_05_Unfair[/link] does heighten during the adolescent period, but it becomes less frequent as adolescence progresses (Shanahan, et al., 2007). The emotional intensity and stakes tend to increase, however, perhaps accounting for the overall perception that adolescence is a period of high discord (De Goede et al., 2009). Given that psychologists have noted the functional role of conflict, it’s reasonable to suspect that this common feature of adolescence might serve a purpose, and that may also help us understand its common sources.</para>
    <para id="para-00012">Adolescents undergo dramatic physical, cognitive, and self- and social redefinition. Parent-adolescent conflict could be both a driver of this redefining of roles and qualities, and a consequence of it (Branje et al., 2012; Collins &amp; Sternberg, 2006). Adolescents’ new reasoning skills, including hypothetical-deductive reasoning and the ability to think abstractly, often lead to the <term id="term-00001">de-idealization</term> of parents. That is, teens see a mismatch between the values their parents proclaim, the rules they put forward, and the way imperfect parents lead their own imperfect lives. Those same reasoning skills, which parents, teachers, and society at large all encourage, can lead adolescents to imagine an alternative world in which the rules are just a bit different, and not so “unfair.”</para>
    <para id="para-00013">Across adolescence, autonomy and self-determination grow, and the roles teenagers take on change as they are granted more responsibility (Mastrotheodoros, et al., 2019). Their changing physicality also means that parents and other adults in the community expect more adult-like behavior from teens, even as we know their frontal lobes are not yet finished developing. When teens fall short, both they and their parents might feel disappointment and frustration.</para>
    <para id="para-00014">Adolescent-parent conflict makes sense, then, when we consider the totality of the psychological and social circumstances in which adolescents find themselves. The negotiation and resolution of this friction can lead to a more egalitarian relationship between parent and child (Branje, 2018). Conflict declines with the parent’s gradual release of control as the adolescent learns to make their way in the larger world. Arguments about household chores, curfews, appearance and dress, use of the family car, and spending money all make sense from this developmental perspective. Allowing teens more freedom within a structured environment can reduce conflict and improve family well-being.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00004">
    <title>Family Structure</title>
    <para id="para-00015">Developmental psychologists have long been interested in the role family structure may play in child and adolescent outcomes. Researchers have investigated a variety of such structures, including two-parent families, single-parent families, multigenerational families, and step- or blended families. As we’ll see, the results are probably due to the different ways family members function and interact with one another rather than to the family structures themselves. Disparities in resources, such as available income and the ability to monitor children, also factor heavily in producing different outcomes.</para>
    <section id="sect-00005">
    <title>Differences in Outcomes</title>
    <para id="para-00016">Decades of research on family structure and outcomes have shown a consistent pattern: children and adolescents in two-parent households fare better across a range of physical and psychological measures of well-being (Savell, et al., 2021; Langton &amp; Berger, 2011; Parache, et al., 2023). A higher percentage of adolescents in both single-parent and stepfamily households exhibit more negative behavioral conduct (such as acting out) and higher levels of depression and anxiety than adolescents in two-parent households (Parche, et al., 2023).</para>
    <para id="para-00017">In a longitudinal study of a large number of German families, adolescents in stepfamilies reported particularly low-quality relationships with their parents, compared to teens in single- or two- parent families (Walper &amp; Beckh, 2010). Similarly, in a longitudinal study of families in the United States, disruptive behavior reported by teachers was lowest for children in two-parent families (Savell, et al., 2021). Another study using a large-scale national survey in the United States found adolescents in two-parent families had better overall physical health than those in all other family structures (Langton &amp; Berger, 2011).</para>
    <para id="para-00018">This pattern was also reported in a recent large-scale national survey of Korean youth (Park &amp; Lee, 2020), with some additional findings: adolescents from families other than two-parent households showed higher levels of risky behavior such as drinking and smoking, higher levels of suicidal ideation, more stress, and lower perceived level of academic performance. In short, the scientific literature has been reporting the comparative benefits of the two-parent family for quite some time. But what is it exactly about family structure that causes these observed outcomes?</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00006">
    <title>Explanations for Different Outcomes</title>
    <para id="para-00019">Recently, scholars have noted that two-parent (sometimes called “intact”) families have enjoyed a type of privilege in both our culture and our scientific investigations. They have sought to identify the links between a family’s composition and the well-being of its members.</para>
    <para id="para-00020">Socioeconomic status is one such link. Studies of families in the United States (Savell, et al. 2021) and Germany (Parache, et al., 2023) found strong associations between SES and adolescent well-being. Two-parent households typically have more income and other resources (such as time) available for benefiting household members. The financial strain that comes from having fewer resources could contribute additional stress in other family structures. Other factors include level of parental monitoring and parental ability to protect family members from adverse health conditions that result in accidents and injury (Langton &amp; Berger, 2011). The negative findings for stepfamilies may seem confusing since these families have two parents. However, they tend to experience lower income levels, greater potential for within-family conflict, and less stability.</para>
    <para id="para-00021">Government policies and programs can be informed by recognizing the role these factors likely play in child and adolescent outcomes. For example, as a society, we may look for ways to support single-parent households financially through tax breaks or other programs in order to increase positive outcomes for children and adolescents living in this common family arrangement.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00007">
    <title>Similar Outcomes</title>
    <para id="para-00022">We’ve now seen that family structure by itself becomes less influential on developmental outcomes when we take SES into account. U.S. researcher Judith Harris (1998, 2009) put forth a further and provocative argument (1998, 2009) when she challenged the <term id="term-00002">nurture assumption</term>, the idea that parents’ socialization of their children plays a primary role in the way the children turn out. Harris argued that when we look at global outcomes such as school achievement, employment history, and mental health outcomes, variations among parents don’t make much difference. Instead, she showed that children from similar social, economic, and physical backgrounds (neighborhoods) turn out remarkably the same. It’s all the shared experiences from growing up in similar circumstances that create similar outcomes later in life.</para>
    <para id="para-00023">In essence, Harris argued that the primary role of parents is to select a neighborhood and provide a particular level of resources (money, time, monitoring). Any differences we then observe in outcomes are likely due to differences in genes. Thus, she argued for a relatively limited role of parents (and families) beyond their initial genetic contributions and provision of resources.</para>
    <para id="para-00024">Harris’s challenge generated years-long debates (Collins et al., 2000; Eagly &amp; Wood, 2013). Developmental psychologists continue to develop ways to tease apart the relative contributions of nature and nurture to child and adolescent outcomes. Psychologists also note the nurture assumption’s emphasis on global outcomes, such as the adolescent’s eventual educational attainment or occupational level. It is obvious that on a different level parents play a large role in nurturing their children toward specific values, beliefs, preferences, practices, and even abilities. As the debate illustrates, parents make important contributions to the well-being of their offspring, providing a set of both genetic and environmental influences.</para>
    </section>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00008">
    <title>Positive Youth Development</title>
    <para id="para-00025">The <term id="term-00003">developmental assets approach</term> establishes optimal conditions for adolescent growth, ensuring that as teens mature, they benefit from a community that values and includes them in decision-making, provides a safe environment, and sets clear expectations and boundaries. This supportive environment allows adolescents to give back to their community and engage in meaningful activities, fostering a sense of spirituality and aligning with like-minded peers. These 20 external assets create a safety net that propels development, while the 20 internal assets represent the individual achievements resulting from this support.</para>
    <para id="para-00026">This approach aligns with the <term id="term-00004">positive youth development (PYD)</term> perspective (Lerner, 2005; Lerner, Dowling &amp; Anderson, 2003; Roth, et al., 1998), which integrates various elements to foster healthy development in children and adolescents. PYD focuses on promoting positive outcomes, even in problem-focused programs, and emphasizes a hopeful environment and opportunities for growth. The “5 Cs” of PYD—competence, character, confidence, connections, and compassion (Lerner, et al., 2005)—are goals for youth development. PYD programs set high expectations for achievement and provide continuous support, mirroring high-quality parenting and educational best practices.</para>
    <section id="sect-00009">
    <title>4-H Programs</title>
    <para id="para-00027">Many dozens of positive youth development programs operate at the state and national levels, and one held in highest regard is 4-H. 4-H is a national youth development program run in association with the extension/outreach divisions of universities around the United States. The four Hs stand for head, heart, hands, and health, and the programs seek to promote clear thought, loyalty, service to the community, and a healthy lifestyle. Currently, more than six million youth participate in 4-H programs (<link url="https://www.4-h.org/about/">www.4-h.org/about/</link>). Programming is diverse and includes clubs that meet regularly, camps, workshops and projects of every kind. A comprehensive evaluation of 4-H (Lerner et al., 2015) identified a number of long-term benefits. Participants are more likely to contribute to their communities throughout high school and showed a higher level of engaged citizenship, decreased risk for problem behaviors (in girls only, no difference for boys), higher academic performance, and a greater tendency to endorse and exhibit health-promoting attitudes and behaviors. Overall, 4-H does not just benefit its youthful participants; it also serves as a pathway for their ongoing contributions to each local community and the broader society.</para>
    <figure id="fig-00002">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!--<image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_10_05_PosYouth.jpg"/>-->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Youth activities that foster positive development are available through various community groups and organizations. (a) Teens learn about the City of Greenville’s video production control room as part of a “Grow Local” initiative that brings high school students to visit various area organizations and businesses in Greenville, North Carolina. (b) This 4-H group helped with trail maintenance and planted a butterfly garden as part of its Outdoor Wildlife Leadership Service project in the Land Between the Lakes National Recreation Area in Kentucky.</caption>
    </figure>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00010">
    <title>Scouting America</title>
    <para id="para-00028">Scouting America is another organization devoted to youth development. Founded in 1910 (as the Boy Scouts of America), it includes more than one million children and adolescents in the United States alone (<link url="https://www.scouting.org">www.scouting.org</link>). The organization is now open to all genders. Through a series of programs that include mentoring, lifelong learning, faith traditions, healthy living, character development, and service to others, the Scouts seeks to encourage youth to try new things, help others, improve self-confidence, and build strong ethical standards.</para>
    <para id="para-00029">Recent studies highlight the positive impact of scouting on youth development. For instance, research found that teens in scouting exhibit higher levels of civic engagement and social responsibility than their non-scouting peers (Kim et al., 2016; Polson et al., 2013). Participants also reported improved mental health and resilience, attributing this to the supportive network and structured activities provided by scouting programs (Wang et al., 2021). These findings emphasize the enduring value of scouting in fostering well-rounded, capable, and community-minded individuals.</para>
    <para id="para-00030">The Girl Scouts youth development programs have similar goals and outcomes. The organization also has more than one million members in the United States (<link url="https://www.girlscouts.orgwww.girlscouts.org">www.girlscouts.orgwww.girlscouts.org</link>) and offers leadership training and experiences for girls and young women. Youth who participate in Girl Scouts programs report feeling more empowered to take action in their communities, exhibit a strong sense of environmental stewardship, and have increased knowledge of financial literacy - the primary aim of the Girl Scout cookies program, among other benefits (Girl Scouts Research Institute, 2014; Modi et al., 2012).</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00011">
    <title>Faith-Based Youth Groups</title>
    <para id="para-00031">Many adolescents also participate in faith-based youth groups. Fifty-one percent of all U.S. teenagers have attended a religious youth group at some point in their lives, while 29 percent currently attend such group meetings regularly (Pew Research Center, 2020). Personal and spiritual development are the twin focus points of faith-based youth group programming. These groups serve an important role in religious socialization of youth, as well as encouraging leadership and a sense of belonging and providing purposeful ways for youth to engage with each other and their community weekly.</para>
    <para id="para-00032">Research has found youth who participate in such programs have higher self-esteem, better social adjustment overall, and lower levels of personal distress and risk-taking behaviors (such as smoking or underage drinking) (Morton &amp; Montgomery, 2011). Being curious about their spirituality and place in the universe is a common characteristic of adolescents, including those who profess no particular religion (“nones”) <link target-id="fig-00003"/>. A recent survey found considerable consistency in this sense of wonder across teens from several major U.S. Christian denominations, as well as for U.S. teens overall (including non-religiously affiliated teens) (Pew Research Group, 2020), highlighting the importance of spirituality in adolescent development.</para>
    <para id="para-00033">A recent study surveyed Singaporean youth and found that individuals with a religion had higher overall positive mental health scores than those without. Religious affiliation was linked to higher scores in spirituality, general coping, emotional support, and interpersonal skills, but not in personal growth, global affect, or mental disorders. Each religion had unique benefits: Christianity was linked to higher emotional support; Taoism, Buddhism, and Islam with better coping and support; Hinduism with better interpersonal skills; and Sikhism with both better support and interpersonal skills. (Vaingankar et al., 2021).</para>
    <figure id="fig-00003">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!--<image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_10_05_Nones.jpg"/>-->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>As the far-right column shows, teens who do not identify themselves with any religion (“nones”) are just as likely as religious teens to report feeling a deep sense of wonder about the universe.</caption>
    </figure>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00012">
    <title>Youth Sports Programs</title>
    <para id="para-00034">Youth sports offer another important context for positive youth development. Among teenagers between 13 and 17 in the United States, regular participation in a team sport, including travel teams (non-school-based teams) was at 36 percent in 2022 (Aspen Institute Project Play, 2023). Youth participation in sports has experienced a 6 percent decline since the COVID-2019 pandemic, however. The Sports &amp; Fitness Industry Association has suggested that youth may be turning to more individual-based activities like videogames, social media, and other types of outdoor activities.</para>
    <para id="para-00035">There has been a slight decline since 2019 for boys and a slight increase for girls’ participation in regular sports. There are roughly similar participation rates across several ethnoracial groups, including White youth (40 percent), Black youth (35 percent), AA/PI youth (37 percent), and Hispanic youth (34 percent). Family income level appears to play a big role in determining participation in youth sports. Forty-three percent of youth in households making more than $100,000 per year participate regularly, whereas just 26 percent of youth from families making less than $25,000 do. This is likely due to many factors including time constraints and the growing expenses for equipment, travel, uniforms, and registration fees associated with youth sports.</para>
    <para id="para-00036">The benefits of participation in sports include better mental health outcomes (reduced anxiety and depression), increased leadership and team-building skills including cooperation and other pro-social behaviors, and positive effects on physical health and overall physical development. The Project Play group reports some negative trends including an increase in certain types of injuries (such as to knees and ankles). The group also notes that many non- school-based sports groups do not have adequate safety policy and protocols in place for their youth participants. In recent years there has also been a decline in the participation and activity levels of youth with disabilities (MacEachern et al., 2022). High schools in the United States have increased their awareness and access to adaptive sports programs, however (Carbone et al., 2021).</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00002">
    <para id="para-00037">Is playing sports as beneficial as commonly believed, or is it primarily entertainment? Watch this TED-Ed talk titled <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104playingsport"><emphasis effect="italics">How playing sports benefits your body ... and your brain</emphasis></link> to learn more.</para>
    </note>
    </section>
    </section>
    <section class="summary" id="sect-00013">
    <list id="list-00002">
    <item>Effective conflict management in teen-parent relationships often hinges on the parenting style. Understanding and adapting parenting practices—such as authoritative or authoritarian—to balance parental guidance and teen independence can significantly enhance the quality of teen-parent relationships during adolescence.</item>
    <item>Positive youth development is a sub-field of developmental psychology and aims to promote or instill healthy psychological functioning in all children and adolescents. Participation in various youth programs can provide structured environments where teens can develop leadership skills, build self-esteem, and foster a sense of community.</item>
    </list>
    </section>
